WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.280
Now, I'd like to introduce our featured presenters today,

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.125
professors Jim Leape and Pamela Matson.

00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:18.440
Jim directs with Fiorenza Micheli,

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.825
the Stanford Center for Ocean Solutions,

00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:23.060
and is the William and Eva Price Fellow

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.890
at the Stanford Woods Institute for the Environment.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.335
Jim has more than three decades of conservation experience,

00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:33.140
spanning a broad range of conservation issues on every continent.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:40.130
From 2005-2014, he served as director general of World Wildlife Fund International,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.045
and leader of the Global World Wildlife Fund Network.

00:00:43.045 --> 00:00:45.570
Before going to World Wildlife Fund International,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.410
Jim directed the conservation and science initiatives

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.380
of the David and Lucile Packard Foundation.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.590
Previously, he served as Executive Vice President of

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.920
World Wildlife Fund US, in Washington DC,

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.360
as a lawyer for the United Nations Environment Programme in Nairobi, Kenya,

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.710
as a law professor,

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:08.300
and as a litigator for the National Audubon Society and for the US Department of Justice.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.305
He is also a distinguished fellow at the Climate Works Foundation.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:16.695
Pamela is an interdisciplinary sustainability scientist,

00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:19.985
academic leader, and organizational strategist.

00:01:19.985 --> 00:01:24.740
A former dean of Stanford University School of Earth Energy and Environmental Sciences,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.630
she is the Goldman Professor of

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.455
Environmental Studies and senior fellow at the Woods Institute for the Environment,

00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:34.475
and leads the graduate program on Sustainability Science and Practice.

00:01:34.475 --> 00:01:38.419
Her research addresses a range of environmental and sustainability issues,

00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:41.360
including sustainability of agricultural systems,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.620
vulnerability and resilience of particular people,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.580
and places to climate change and characteristics of

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.285
science that can contribute to sustainability transitions at scale.

00:01:50.285 --> 00:01:54.425
Some of her over 200 publications include: Seeds of Sustainability:

00:01:54.425 --> 00:01:58.910
Lessons From the Birthplace of the Green Revolution and Pursuing Sustainability.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.970
She's an elected member of the National Academy of Science

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.565
and the American Academy of Arts and Sciences,

00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:09.110
and received a MacArthur Foundation Award among many other awards and recognitions.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:13.310
With that, let's turn it over to Jim and Pamela.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.310
Good morning everyone, thank you for joining us for

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.135
our strategies for sustainability program.

00:02:19.135 --> 00:02:23.315
Today, we're going to be talking about a really important type

00:02:23.315 --> 00:02:27.665
of social capital that allows us to address challenges,

00:02:27.665 --> 00:02:34.310
very complex challenges that span sectors and span across spatial scales.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.980
But before we start that conversation,

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.985
I think it makes sense to take a moment and ask,

00:02:39.985 --> 00:02:42.085
what does sustainability mean?

00:02:42.085 --> 00:02:44.660
What do we mean by sustainability?

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.505
Of course, this is a term that's used by different people in different ways, but, um,

00:02:49.505 --> 00:02:52.315
it's very commonly used in the corporate world,

00:02:52.315 --> 00:02:54.710
and their corporate sustainability is

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.635
about a triple bottom line or the three-legged stool.

00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:01.160
It's about making decisions that make sense economically,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.120
but also socially, and environmentally.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:10.860
And today ESG is a central part of many corporations, ah, business.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.190
Why do cor- why do corporations do it?

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.560
I should just say, it's partly because it saves money, it reduces risk,

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.970
helps recruit and retain the best of the, um, uh,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:28.300
employees, and also is because corporations care about their communities in the world.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:33.890
And the other organizations that use sustainability regularly is,

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.600
The International Development Community.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.110
The, the community that's trying to help some of the poorest places on

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.280
the world- in the world develop socially and economically.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.010
Over three decades ago,

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.960
it was recognized that if we want to help meet the needs of,

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.125
of the present, we have to do so without compromising the future.

00:03:54.125 --> 00:03:57.180
So sustainable development became a major focus,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.745
and it's been worked on for many many years with quite a bit of success.

00:04:01.745 --> 00:04:06.290
Today, the world is focusing on the 17 SDGs,

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.525
or sustainable development goals of the UN.

00:04:08.525 --> 00:04:12.840
Corporations, uh, governments, universities,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.310
and other research organizations, NGOs,

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:20.110
foundations are all working together to try to address these goals.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.275
What do they have in common?

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:26.255
They both, both uses of the term sustainability and others

00:04:26.255 --> 00:04:30.890
are focusing on the well-being of people, and organizations.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.470
But not just today,

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:33.915
also in the future,

00:04:33.915 --> 00:04:35.895
and not just here where we live,

00:04:35.895 --> 00:04:37.665
but all around the world.

00:04:37.665 --> 00:04:40.555
And because of that common focus,

00:04:40.555 --> 00:04:44.740
I'd like to think about sustainability as, ah,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:49.745
sustainability goal as that of inter-generational social well-being.

00:04:49.745 --> 00:04:53.165
So if we're using the word well-being of people,

00:04:53.165 --> 00:04:54.725
what do we mean by that?

00:04:54.725 --> 00:04:57.710
There's lots of different, uh, uh,

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.130
discussions about what is included in the term well-being,

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:05.930
but certainly it includes meeting the material needs of people for food and energy,

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.220
and water, and shelter,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.870
and employment and so forth.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:14.330
It means, um, addressing health issues and meeting educational needs,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.510
and providing opportunities for people to choose, for example,

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:21.840
what they do with their life an- and security and so on.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:27.680
If you think about how hard it is to achieve those,

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.380
that goal of inter-generational well-being,

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:32.820
the well-being of people not just today, but in the future,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.940
you recognize the huge challenges,

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:40.650
but many of us also recognized the huge opportunities in doing so.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:49.580
But it is not easy and the problem here is that these kinds of

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.925
questions that we're addressing for sustainability and

00:05:52.925 --> 00:05:57.425
for meeting the inter-generational well-being of people around the world,

00:05:57.425 --> 00:06:00.454
um, are, are really complex problems,

00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:03.440
and they take place in highly complex systems.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.985
There's lots of different players,

00:06:04.985 --> 00:06:06.605
lots of different moving parts,

00:06:06.605 --> 00:06:11.690
lots of interactions, feedbacks, non-linearities.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.510
And the fact is,

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:16.470
intervening in one area may,

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.225
may have unintended consequences for other parts of the system.

00:06:20.225 --> 00:06:23.660
So changing a policy could have impacts

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:27.140
in many places that you didn't expect them to occur,

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.535
or a new technology could also have unintended consequences.

00:06:31.535 --> 00:06:33.740
So because of this complexity, um,

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.620
[NOISE] we believe that it's very

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.480
helpful to use systems approaches to do systems thinking,

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:45.590
and to utilize frameworks to help us design and

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:50.710
implement actions that address the goals of sustainability despite complexity.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.190
So one such framework is this shown here, at,

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:57.110
at the top you see well-being,

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.490
the constituents of well-being,

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:00.260
we talked about before.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:05.120
There are lots of production and consumption processes that we and you,

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.105
and all of us who are actors and agents of change,

00:07:08.105 --> 00:07:13.190
are carrying out in trying- in trying to address that well-being goal.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:15.600
We could spend hours, days,

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.330
years talking about how those production and consumption processes could change,

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:23.900
but today let's just turn to the fact that they, there,

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:28.380
they rest on five major asset groups.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.290
These underlie our ability to produce and consume well-being,

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:37.295
they underlie our ability to meet that well-being challenge.

00:07:37.295 --> 00:07:40.100
So start with human capital.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:44.270
This is just the numbers of people or demographics,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.640
um, the- the health of people,

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.070
the accumulated knowledge that people have,

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:54.455
and these are the things that allow humans to interact in complicated systems.

00:07:54.455 --> 00:07:58.790
Social capital includes the rules and regulations,

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:02.900
the culture and cultural and social norms, uh,

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.510
the governance systems, the networks,

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.000
and the partnerships that allow us to work

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.575
together as we try to reach the sustainability goal.

00:08:12.575 --> 00:08:16.220
Manufactured capital includes everything we make,

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.790
it's our roads and our airports,

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.535
and our energy plants,

00:08:20.535 --> 00:08:24.070
it's our factories and the things that we make in those factories,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:28.130
it's the new technologies that we're developing including, uh, you know,

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:31.415
our- our- our hand-held devices,

00:08:31.415 --> 00:08:35.000
it's books, it's management practices,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.680
anything that we make.

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:41.105
Natural capital includes everything that this planet offers us.

00:08:41.105 --> 00:08:43.880
It includes ecosystems, on land and in

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.030
the oceans that provide so many of the things we need.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:51.020
It includes: water resources and soil resources, our climate system,

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:55.035
our atmosphere system, and minerals that we draw on,

00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:56.280
and many other things.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.350
And then finally, the fifth major group of [NOISE] assets is knowledge capital.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.795
This includes, the knowledge that we gained from scientific research,

00:09:05.795 --> 00:09:08.740
but it also includes the knowledge we gain from,

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:13.330
from practice, from experience, from traditional knowledge.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:19.100
So all of these things work together to underpin our efforts towards sustainability,

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.900
and if one of them is missing,

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.945
we're not going to probably make much progress.

00:09:23.945 --> 00:09:25.915
In some places for example,

00:09:25.915 --> 00:09:28.455
what's missing is the rule of law,

00:09:28.455 --> 00:09:32.495
and it gets in the way of communities trying to move towards sustainability.

00:09:32.495 --> 00:09:34.975
In other areas, it might be infrastructure.

00:09:34.975 --> 00:09:38.150
In other areas, it might be depleted natural resources.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:40.205
We need them all, and in many cases,

00:09:40.205 --> 00:09:41.975
we have to develop them,

00:09:41.975 --> 00:09:45.560
grow them so that we can meet our sustainability goals.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:47.180
You have to pay attention to all of them.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:50.430
Today, we're going to focus on social capital,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.860
and on one particular type of social capitals,

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:55.995
rather new rather challenging,

00:09:55.995 --> 00:10:01.615
and that is the use of partnerships to address really really complicated problems.

00:10:01.615 --> 00:10:03.145
And to present this,

00:10:03.145 --> 00:10:08.810
Jim Leape is going to take us through some cases and some experience that he's had,

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.990
as both a developer of these kinds of partnerships and someone

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:15.495
who's analyzing how well they work So Jim over to you.

00:10:15.495 --> 00:10:17.805
Great. Thanks Pam.

00:10:17.805 --> 00:10:23.060
As we, uh, live in an increasingly globalized economy, um,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.805
the creation of social capital becomes evermore important,

00:10:25.805 --> 00:10:28.235
and in many ways evermore challenging.

00:10:28.235 --> 00:10:31.385
Um, and that's what we want to talk about a little bit, um, this morning.

00:10:31.385 --> 00:10:33.735
Um, Is how do you create social capital,

00:10:33.735 --> 00:10:35.935
in a globalized economic sector.

00:10:35.935 --> 00:10:39.845
As all of us demand more and more from the Earth, um,

00:10:39.845 --> 00:10:44.060
we're putting more and more pressure on producers to expand production

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:48.765
of commodities such as soy or beef or seafood or copper,

00:10:48.765 --> 00:10:51.105
uh, and as they expand production,

00:10:51.105 --> 00:10:54.070
they are putting more and more pressure on the natural resources,

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:56.460
the natural capital that supports us all.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:01.585
Now in the context of fierce global competition in global markets,

00:11:01.585 --> 00:11:05.840
it's often difficult for any one country to control those forces,

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:08.240
and to manage its own resources successfully.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.640
And so a success will come from thinking

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.010
much more broadly about what kinds of collaborations,

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:14.344
what kinds of partnerships,

00:11:14.344 --> 00:11:16.130
can actually drive change.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.680
And this is a complex of ideas and possibilities that

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.550
some have called ecosystems of change.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:26.260
It recognizes that in a globalized economy change comes from diverse actors,

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:30.585
not just governments, not just companies, but also NGOs,

00:11:30.585 --> 00:11:33.945
civil society organizations of other kinds, communities,

00:11:33.945 --> 00:11:40.430
international organizations, engaged with each other in an evolving set of interactions.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.340
So those are interactions that may take one form early in

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:46.420
a change process then evolve significantly over time,

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.870
as these collaborators interact.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:53.850
Um, and together are finding ways to create solutions that can meet these challenges,

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:56.470
and to drive those solutions into implementation

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:59.665
on a scale that really will change outcomes.

00:11:59.665 --> 00:12:03.270
Now for all of us the touchstone in these efforts

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:06.540
is the sustainable development goals which provide us for the first time,

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.030
a global agenda for sustainability.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:12.150
And these goals have become increasingly a touchstone

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:15.240
not only for government actors but also for many companies,

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.295
and for civil society.

00:12:17.295 --> 00:12:19.290
One of those goals, um,

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:21.615
is the sustainable management of forests.

00:12:21.615 --> 00:12:25.650
Ah, it's this goal that I want to focus on, ah, this morning.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:28.080
Uh, this goal you will note,

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.570
um, calls among its many targets,

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:34.515
for an end to deforestation globally by 2020,

00:12:34.515 --> 00:12:36.615
which is after all, uh, next year.

00:12:36.615 --> 00:12:40.955
Um, and it's worth noting as we talk about collaborations in this space.

00:12:40.955 --> 00:12:44.305
Um, but we are on a track to achieve that goal, in fact,

00:12:44.305 --> 00:12:49.725
we saw a record levels of deforestation around the world in 2016 and 2017.

00:12:49.725 --> 00:12:53.385
Um, but this is the target we've set ourselves and it is the touchstone,

00:12:53.385 --> 00:12:56.570
for companies and civil society and others to come

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:00.810
together around trying to change the direction of those curves.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:04.740
The big problem is clearing of forests for agriculture.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.950
Seventy one percent of tropical and subtropical deforestation

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:11.325
comes from conversion of forest lands to agriculture.

00:13:11.325 --> 00:13:14.500
And together, agriculture and deforestation account for

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.970
nearly a quarter of greenhouse gas emissions globally.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:19.920
So this is a big problem from

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:23.565
a forest-wise perspective but also from a climate perspective.

00:13:23.565 --> 00:13:26.770
One of the biggest culprits in driving deforestation is

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.460
palm oil especially in Southeast Asia.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:32.165
And I'm going to focus on palm oil as a case study,

00:13:32.165 --> 00:13:34.595
to look at how partnerships are beginning to form,

00:13:34.595 --> 00:13:37.320
and where they might go from here.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:42.180
We saw explosive growth in palm oil production over the last half century,

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.760
starting from virtually nothing in the mid '60s,

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.170
you see in the red line,

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:48.975
the growth of palm oil production first in Malaysia,

00:13:48.975 --> 00:13:52.170
and then maybe 10 years later in Indonesia.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:54.390
Indonesia itself alone accounts for

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:58.605
more than half of all global palm oil production today.

00:13:58.605 --> 00:14:02.040
And this is a product which you rarely know you are buying,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:04.560
but it is in half the products in the grocery store.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.775
It's in little tiny type on the back of each package,

00:14:07.775 --> 00:14:12.050
whether it is lipstick or cup noodles or peanut butter,

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:14.960
cup noodles [LAUGHTER] or peanut butter,

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:17.040
or soap or shampoo,

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.005
or many other products.

00:14:19.005 --> 00:14:22.365
Um, and so it is on the one hand ubiquitous,

00:14:22.365 --> 00:14:24.570
on the one hand hugely significant,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.465
the most important vegetable oil on the global markets,

00:14:27.465 --> 00:14:30.875
at the same time a very difficult challenge to tackle,

00:14:30.875 --> 00:14:32.855
because nobody knows they're eating palm oil,

00:14:32.855 --> 00:14:34.850
they don't think about buying palm oil.

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:37.550
And so for many years it was very hard to get,

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:39.675
um, at this problem.

00:14:39.675 --> 00:14:42.120
And then in 2001, Migros,

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:44.550
which some of you from Europe will know is,

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:48.045
um, is a grocery chain in Switzerland,

00:14:48.045 --> 00:14:51.645
Migros came to WWF and said,

00:14:51.645 --> 00:14:54.255
How can we work together to address this problem

00:14:54.255 --> 00:14:58.020
of unsustainable production of palm oil globally?

00:14:58.020 --> 00:14:59.865
And out of that process,

00:14:59.865 --> 00:15:01.290
out of that first engagement,

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:05.760
came a small collaboration with a handful of retailers,

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:10.050
and producers, and NGOs, Civil society organizations,

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.605
who came together to- to begin to develop

00:15:12.605 --> 00:15:15.135
principles for sustainable production of palm oil.

00:15:15.135 --> 00:15:19.700
And so was created the round table on sustainable palm oil, RSPO.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:24.660
And RSPO, those parties and a growing number of stakeholders from each sector,

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:27.390
worked together over the years that followed to

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:31.110
develop a standard for sustainability in that sector.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.540
Now if you think about it for a minute you realize that of

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:35.690
course that's a daunting undertaking, um,

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:40.155
because having all the stakeholders together is essential for credibility,

00:15:40.155 --> 00:15:42.335
and could be essential for impact,

00:15:42.335 --> 00:15:47.320
but it also makes it very difficult to get to agreement on what a standard should be.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.640
And I think it's clear that RSPO has

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.925
worked very hard over the last 20 years or the last 18 years,

00:15:53.925 --> 00:15:55.595
to develop a credible standard.

00:15:55.595 --> 00:15:58.035
It has made huge progress in that time.

00:15:58.035 --> 00:16:01.970
And it has also demonstrated that progress is challenging.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:06.270
That is almost inevitably too slow for some actors,

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:09.065
and too fast or too strict for others.

00:16:09.065 --> 00:16:12.740
And RSPO has been trying to wrestle those challenges to the ground.

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:17.775
Um, it has come up with a standard that is increasingly the touchstone in this sector.

00:16:17.775 --> 00:16:22.259
But while RSPO wrestled with those difficult problems,

00:16:22.259 --> 00:16:26.700
two other insights really changed the way civil society and

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:31.505
companies began to think about how- how change could happen in this sector.

00:16:31.505 --> 00:16:34.245
And the first was a recognition coming out of WWF.

00:16:34.245 --> 00:16:37.620
That they are in fact even in these huge global sectors,

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:41.550
keystone actors who really can be crucial in driving change.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:44.375
So if you take the commodity sectors global,

00:16:44.375 --> 00:16:46.085
not just palm oil but also soy,

00:16:46.085 --> 00:16:47.190
and beef, and tuna,

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.640
and shrimp, you take those global commodity sectors broadly,

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:53.655
they of course have now seven-plus billion consumers.

00:16:53.655 --> 00:16:56.895
All of us are consumers of products that come out of those sectors.

00:16:56.895 --> 00:17:00.000
And they have maybe 1-1.5 billion producers,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.310
fishermen, and farmers who are growing crops or catching fish around the world.

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:06.250
That's a pret- those are pretty big numbers,

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.495
pretty hard to influence directly,

00:17:08.495 --> 00:17:13.530
the behaviors or practices of 1.5 billion farmers and fishermen.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.240
But there is connecting those two groups,

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:18.000
connecting producers to buyers,

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:22.560
there is a supply chain that actually has a very pronounced choke point.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.320
And there are about by [inaudible] estimate.

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:27.210
If you look across 15 commodities that are

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:30.935
the most important commodities for driving biodiversity loss around the world,

00:17:30.935 --> 00:17:33.630
there are about 500 companies that control

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:37.185
70 percent of the production and trade in those sectors.

00:17:37.185 --> 00:17:40.270
And that begins to become a very ma- manageable number.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:41.890
That if you can reach those companies,

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:44.415
engage them supporting change,

00:17:44.415 --> 00:17:47.835
you can begin to make a difference that matters globally.

00:17:47.835 --> 00:17:49.740
And the second insight, um,

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:51.840
in this example comes from Oxfam,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:55.639
the second insight is that many of those big companies have brands,

00:17:55.639 --> 00:17:58.320
consumer facing brands, and they are often

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:03.345
highly focused on the integrity and reputation of those brands.

00:18:03.345 --> 00:18:05.230
And that means that NGOs,

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:07.955
civil society organizations especially campaigners,

00:18:07.955 --> 00:18:11.345
can engage companies by engaging their brands.

00:18:11.345 --> 00:18:15.150
And so take two examples in the case of palm oil. One is Unilever.

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:19.620
Unilever has for a very long time been the world's largest buyer of palm oil.

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:23.020
It's central to soap and to margarine two of their core products,

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:25.550
but many other things they make as well.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:28.150
And Unilever makes Dove beauty products.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.700
And for many years their campaign, ah,

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:34.540
in supportive Dove beauty products has been the campaign for real beauty,

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:36.920
of which this ad is one example.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:40.150
And so in 2008 Greenpeace took off on this ad,

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:43.650
to bring home the message that that Unilever was

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:47.990
buying palm oil and therefore was part of driving deforestation.

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:51.540
And this is an ad that hit Unilever pretty hard.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:55.545
And this is an ad that goes to the direct directly to their brand positioning,

00:18:55.545 --> 00:19:00.205
to their reputation, and the reputation that they have earned and continue to build.

00:19:00.205 --> 00:19:02.220
And so within a few days,

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.280
Green- Unilever sat down with Greenpeace to begin to talk about,

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:09.910
okay how do they shift their purchasing to sustainably produce palm oil?

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:12.375
Greenpeace did the same thing with Nestle.

00:19:12.375 --> 00:19:18.215
Nestle of course one of the most widely known brands in the food sector,

00:19:18.215 --> 00:19:21.810
an iconic brand for Nestle is KitKat.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:27.930
And if you haven't seen it it's worth going on YouTube and asking for Greenpeace KitKat,

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:30.500
you will get the video that lies behind this ad.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:33.805
And what that video brought home in a very vivid way,

00:19:33.805 --> 00:19:35.210
was that every time you, uh,

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:38.540
eat a KitKat bar you are part of deforestation.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:41.210
That Nestle was part of buying palm oil and

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:45.010
therefore contributing to deforestation in Southeast Asia.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:52.810
This hit Nestle hard and Nestle pushed back for two or three or four weeks on social media.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:58.030
Then Greenpeace activists dressed as orangutans dropped out of the ceiling of the uh,

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:00.265
Nestle annual general meeting, um,

00:20:00.265 --> 00:20:03.835
and brought home the- this connection, brought home this issue.

00:20:03.835 --> 00:20:07.120
Talking to colleagues within Nestle and one thing that becomes clear is that

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:10.330
this initially felt like an attack on the company and of course it was.

00:20:10.330 --> 00:20:14.410
But what they realized is that it also was a statement that Greenpeace, sorry,

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:16.330
that Nestle was part of deforestation and that

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.060
is not something Nestle wanted to be a part of.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:20.440
So in the weeks that follow,

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:22.345
Nestle also made a commitment, uh,

00:20:22.345 --> 00:20:24.985
to addressing deforestation in the supply chain.

00:20:24.985 --> 00:20:27.280
And it has worked very intensively over

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:31.270
the last several years to deliver on that commitment.

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:33.805
So you have a couple of big companies, um,

00:20:33.805 --> 00:20:35.770
that have begun to make commitments, that have said,

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:40.450
"We're going to address sustainability of palm oil production in our own supply chains."

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:42.085
But as they did that,

00:20:42.085 --> 00:20:44.125
one thing became clear which is that,

00:20:44.125 --> 00:20:46.750
it is very hard to do it as a single company,

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:53.200
to drive that kind of change as a single actor in a global and complicated, uh, sector.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:56.815
And so Unilever and Nestle and

00:20:56.815 --> 00:21:01.645
others began to realize that they needed to collaborate with their competitors.

00:21:01.645 --> 00:21:05.260
So having been engaged with NGOs like Greenpeace and then

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:09.085
with WWF and others to figure out how to address this problem,

00:21:09.085 --> 00:21:12.220
they realized that everybody would be better off if

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:15.460
the whole consumer product sector move together.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:19.780
If, if Unilever and Nestle and other first-movers

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.435
were joined by other companies in their sector.

00:21:22.435 --> 00:21:27.070
And that would be better for the forest because you'd get broader change,

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:29.485
a broader commitment to sustainability,

00:21:29.485 --> 00:21:32.050
but it will also be better for each of them because they

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:34.945
reduce the risk that by moving on this issue,

00:21:34.945 --> 00:21:37.240
they created a competitive disadvantage,

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:41.215
um, in the marketplace because others could buy palm oil more cheaply.

00:21:41.215 --> 00:21:43.660
And so Unilever, in particular,

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:48.175
led the creation of a commitment in the Consumer Goods Forum to addressing deforestation.

00:21:48.175 --> 00:21:51.460
Consumer Goods Forum which is about 400 companies adding

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:54.940
up to more than $2 trillion in revenues,

00:21:54.940 --> 00:22:01.030
um, who work together on issues of concern to the consumer products in retail sectors.

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:06.220
In 2010, this Consumer Goods Forum committed to addressing deforestation and

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:08.710
the companies- in companies of Consumer Goods Forum

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:12.295
committed to eliminating deforestation from their supply chains,

00:22:12.295 --> 00:22:16.170
focusing in particular on palm oil, also soy, beef,

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:18.450
and timber and paper production which are

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:21.320
the big drivers of deforestation around the world.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:24.385
So for the first time, you have the whole sector moving and

00:22:24.385 --> 00:22:27.520
saying we need together to be coming to grips with these problems.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:31.120
A new kind of collaboration among companies that otherwise can

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:35.575
meet- compete very vigorously in the marketplace.

00:22:35.575 --> 00:22:37.900
Okay. So that gives you the ultimate buyers,

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:39.460
the- the consumer products companies and

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:42.220
the retailers who are selling the ultimate products,

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:44.815
but all of them were supplied by traders.

00:22:44.815 --> 00:22:48.265
And traders of course are largely behind the scenes.

00:22:48.265 --> 00:22:50.935
Traders are companies like Wilmar and Cargill,

00:22:50.935 --> 00:22:54.865
Archer Daniel's Midland who trade vast quantities of commodity,

00:22:54.865 --> 00:22:59.380
who have for years traded commodities largely on the basis of quality and

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:01.750
price because that's how those global markets

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.840
work into our largely out of the public eye.

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:07.810
But as you saw more and more buyers,

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:12.505
more and more of their customers making commitments to eliminating deforestation.

00:23:12.505 --> 00:23:18.190
As you saw the deforestation issue rise more and more on the public agenda,

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:22.330
with campaigns against some of those buyers and with broader campaigns.

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:25.600
Then the traders began to- to engage in this process.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:30.130
They were hearing it from their customers that they needed a deforestation free supply,

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:33.010
they were hearing from campaign organizations and others that

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:36.025
this was a cause they needed to join.

00:23:36.025 --> 00:23:39.295
And Wilmar, the largest single trader in palm oil, uh,

00:23:39.295 --> 00:23:43.540
in 2013 announced that it was committing to be part of addressing

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:48.040
this problem and that it would eliminate deforestation from its supply chains,

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:50.620
um, over the years that followed.

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:54.520
So there you have it, you have the big buyers committed,

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:57.775
you have one of the biggest traders committed,

00:23:57.775 --> 00:24:00.280
and then in 2014 the secretary general of

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.345
the United Nations convened a Climate Summit in New York.

00:24:04.345 --> 00:24:09.235
Um, it was aimed at mobilizing action by actors across the spectrum,

00:24:09.235 --> 00:24:11.380
by companies, by national governments,

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:15.220
by sub-national governments to address the challenge of climate change,

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:19.705
hoping to build momentum going into the Paris negotiations a year later.

00:24:19.705 --> 00:24:21.745
And at this climate summit,

00:24:21.745 --> 00:24:23.530
through the work of, uh,

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:29.230
NGOs in Indonesia, of the Chamber of Commerce in Indonesia, of buying companies,

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:34.254
trading companies, you saw the four largest producers of palm oil in Indonesia,

00:24:34.254 --> 00:24:38.395
and remember Indonesia accounts for more than half of all global production,

00:24:38.395 --> 00:24:41.980
you saw the four largest producers of palm oil in Indonesia step

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:45.385
up and say they too we're going to be part of this movement,

00:24:45.385 --> 00:24:50.125
that they would commit to eliminating deforestation from their production.

00:24:50.125 --> 00:24:54.085
So you get to 2014 that's well

00:24:54.085 --> 00:24:59.065
13 years after Migros and WWF started the round table on sustainable palm oil,

00:24:59.065 --> 00:25:02.575
that's six years after the first campaign against Unilever.

00:25:02.575 --> 00:25:08.350
Twenty fourteen you have 60-70 some would guess 80 percent of the sector,

00:25:08.350 --> 00:25:11.320
that is companies that account for 60 percent, 70 percent,

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:17.440
80 percent of production committed to trade- eliminating deforestation from this sector.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:20.200
And that's a pretty remarkable moment, uh,

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:23.680
pretty remarkable to think that those companies,

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:27.025
that those actors and NGOs could all come together around

00:25:27.025 --> 00:25:32.590
this cause in such a complicated and large economic sector.

00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:36.430
And that led to the creation of something called the Tropical Forest Alliance.

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:40.270
Actually formally announced in 2012 and then expanded in the years that followed.

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:44.590
But a recognition that a pre-competitive collaboration among companies was crucial,

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:48.775
but that actually this- this is a cause that required all hands on deck,

00:25:48.775 --> 00:25:52.390
that this required a collaboration among those companies,

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:55.390
among buyers and traders and producers,

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:59.560
but also that they be joined by civil society organizations,

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:04.150
both those who campaign on these issues and those who provide,

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:06.880
who engage with companies to help them figure out how to solve

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:10.390
them and it required participation by governments,

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:13.750
by governments of importing countries and by in

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:17.560
particular governments of producing countries.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:20.590
And so for example, the Tropical Forest Alliance was joined

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:23.785
formally by the governments of Malaysia and Indonesia,

00:26:23.785 --> 00:26:27.400
the two most important producers of palm oil as part of an effort

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:31.315
to broaden this collaboration and to find a way to make progress together.

00:26:31.315 --> 00:26:37.255
At the same time you see technology come in and technology really will-

00:26:37.255 --> 00:26:40.030
is already and will be increasingly fundamentally

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:43.960
important in creating the dynamics around these kinds of collaborations.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:46.630
Um, Because as we know- as we get

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:49.840
more and more granular information about what's happening on the ground

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:51.310
and that information is

00:26:51.310 --> 00:26:55.780
more and more real-time information or at least timely information,

00:26:55.780 --> 00:27:00.489
then companies and governments and communities are more accountable,

00:27:00.489 --> 00:27:04.930
and they are more able to take action that- on problems as they arise.

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:07.210
So for example, Global Forest Watch created by

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.300
the World Resources Institute developed something called the Palm

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:13.120
Oil Risk Tool which allowed- it made it

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:15.940
possible to identify risks in the palm oil supply chain,

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:19.165
where there were high levels of deforestation or fire.

00:27:19.165 --> 00:27:24.130
Um, also create a facility that would allow- that would send you an alert,

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:27.610
whether you are a government official or a company or an NGO,

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:32.350
could send you an alert if a fire was spotted or forest was being cleared.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:34.990
And these tools get more and more powerful every

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:38.140
year and make it more and more and more and more

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:40.390
possible to actually come to grips with these problems

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:44.380
together and hold each other accountable for progress.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:48.100
Now, that's the story that took us up through 2014.

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:50.200
Um, you saw growing collaboration,

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.710
growing engagement among these different actors in trying to

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:57.700
come to grips with the challenge of deforestation on the ground in the palm oil sector.

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:02.530
And then in 2015 we had an El Nino year and crisis hit.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:05.080
In that year, in August, September,

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:11.080
October there were more than a 100,000 fires across Sumatra and Borneo,

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:13.930
the two largest islands of Indonesia.

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:18.955
And those 100,000 fires were almost without exception intentionally set,

00:28:18.955 --> 00:28:22.160
in most cases to clear land for agriculture.

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:27.090
But what happens in an El Nino year is that fires once set often burn out of

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:32.040
control and many of these lands have deep reservoirs of peat soil,

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:38.175
meters deep which is coal in the making and which can burn and burn and burn forever.

00:28:38.175 --> 00:28:41.460
And so fires once started often just burn for weeks and

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:45.465
months and were very difficult to point- to put out.

00:28:45.465 --> 00:28:49.945
Um, these fires were a climate catastrophe,

00:28:49.945 --> 00:28:52.900
in the months of September and October 2015,

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:55.390
on most days, all those yellow bars,

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:57.730
on most days the emissions from these fires

00:28:57.730 --> 00:29:00.925
exceeded the total daily emissions from the US.

00:29:00.925 --> 00:29:06.310
So a huge amount of carbon put into the atmosphere over the course of these fires.

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:11.165
But these fires were also a public health and development catastrophe.

00:29:11.165 --> 00:29:15.885
So the- the Harvard School of Public Health has estimated more than 100,000 people

00:29:15.885 --> 00:29:20.700
will have shorter lives because of their exposure to the smoke from these fires,

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:24.000
I was in Singapore during the time of these fires.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:27.720
While the air on those days was not too bad in Singapore,

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:30.315
in Malaysia the average concentrations of

00:29:30.315 --> 00:29:33.570
small and fine particulates were about 300 parts per million.

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:35.765
The safety standard is a hundred.

00:29:35.765 --> 00:29:40.195
So the average level in Malaysia was 300 and they had closed all the schools.

00:29:40.195 --> 00:29:42.205
In some parts of Kalimantan,

00:29:42.205 --> 00:29:45.310
the levels reached as high as 1,500 parts per million,

00:29:45.310 --> 00:29:47.605
so 15 times a safe level.

00:29:47.605 --> 00:29:49.840
So this was from a public health perspective,

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:54.625
never mind the climate impacts from a plu- public health perspective, a disaster.

00:29:54.625 --> 00:29:57.610
It was also a development disaster.

00:29:57.610 --> 00:30:01.480
The World Bank estimates that Indonesia took a 2 percent loss in

00:30:01.480 --> 00:30:05.965
global domes- domestic product because of the damage of these fires.

00:30:05.965 --> 00:30:08.950
And so all of a sudden you see all of these issues

00:30:08.950 --> 00:30:11.815
come together concerned about forest protection,

00:30:11.815 --> 00:30:14.844
about climates, but also about health and development,

00:30:14.844 --> 00:30:18.950
all converged in the shared recognition that somehow

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:23.760
we collectively had to come to grips with the challenge of fire and deforestation,

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:26.280
uh, in- for palm oil production.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:28.425
And so in that year,

00:30:28.425 --> 00:30:31.475
President Jokowi shown here in this picture,

00:30:31.475 --> 00:30:34.255
began to put in place restrictions, um,

00:30:34.255 --> 00:30:38.290
to try and ramp up the government of Indonesia's efforts to address

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:43.060
the challenge of palm oil and in particular the associated challenge of fires.

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:48.430
Um, he impo- imposed a moratorium on the conversion of peat lands for example,

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:51.400
put in place a variety of other restrictions.

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:54.475
And so then you had governments more actively

00:30:54.475 --> 00:30:59.290
engaged in this broad effort of coming to grips with this problem.

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:02.155
Now, there is hope on the horizon.

00:31:02.155 --> 00:31:06.520
Um, last year, we saw a down tick in primary forests lost in Indonesia.

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:09.970
You see that it's been quite persistent over the last several years,

00:31:09.970 --> 00:31:12.850
um, as demand for palm oil,

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:15.505
um, continues to put pressure on those forests.

00:31:15.505 --> 00:31:16.945
It dropped last year.

00:31:16.945 --> 00:31:18.970
Last year was a wetter year.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:21.880
It wasn't as dry as 2015 and 2016.

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:26.185
So there's no question that weather helps reduce the level of, uh,

00:31:26.185 --> 00:31:31.255
deforestation by keeping, by making it less likely that fires, er, will spread.

00:31:31.255 --> 00:31:34.390
But I think it's also generally agreed that the actions taken to

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:37.300
def- to reduce deforestation also played a role in,

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:39.490
in beginning to bring that curve down.

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:42.100
I don't think anybody would say,

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:44.710
um, that we have now crafted the problem,

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:46.030
that we have turned the corner,

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:48.490
um, that the challenge of

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:52.705
deforestation in Indonesia and more broadly is behind us, far from it.

00:31:52.705 --> 00:31:56.950
Um, but I think we are seeing that the kinds of

00:31:56.950 --> 00:32:00.880
partnerships it takes to drive chains have been building,

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:04.300
uh, and the focus now is shifting to hadn't take

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:08.965
those global engagements and translate them to concrete change on the ground.

00:32:08.965 --> 00:32:14.080
Uh, and here I think is an important part of thinking about social capital and

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:16.030
thinking about how social capital in

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:19.690
a global sector translates into real change in the forest.

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:21.835
Um, and that is a recognition that,

00:32:21.835 --> 00:32:25.445
that those global commitments are the foundation for change.

00:32:25.445 --> 00:32:27.790
But then you have to get down to the nitty gritty of

00:32:27.790 --> 00:32:30.355
actually implementing change in real places.

00:32:30.355 --> 00:32:32.965
And that's manifest now in, in, uh,

00:32:32.965 --> 00:32:36.310
in an array of efforts that are all under the umbrella of what's called

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:41.335
the jurisdictional approach which is buyers and producers,

00:32:41.335 --> 00:32:45.025
NGOs, communities, and local governments.

00:32:45.025 --> 00:32:47.380
In Indonesia, it's the cabinet buttons,

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:48.850
the district governments within

00:32:48.850 --> 00:32:53.260
a larger province who are working together to figure out concretely,

00:32:53.260 --> 00:32:56.260
how do we end deforestation and fires in

00:32:56.260 --> 00:33:00.755
this jurisdiction and what are each of us going to do to help make that happen.

00:33:00.755 --> 00:33:06.100
And that allows everybody to get to the specifics of what it really takes.

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:09.850
It allows and it calls upon companies and

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:13.945
NGOs to convert a commitment to conservation which

00:33:13.945 --> 00:33:17.200
sustainability into a compelling development proposition to

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:21.760
make sure that this is a movement that really does work for small producers,

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:24.115
large producers, local governments,

00:33:24.115 --> 00:33:27.535
communities in the places where palm oil is grown.

00:33:27.535 --> 00:33:30.250
It is deeply challenging.

00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:33.670
Um, these are hard problems and it is hard to affect change,

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:37.700
uh, in a sector like this globally but even at that local level.

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:40.800
But I think there's broad agreement that that's where the future lies [NOISE] ,

00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:43.380
that you need these global commitments as foundation

00:33:43.380 --> 00:33:46.530
and as creating the ecosystem within which change

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:49.260
can happen and need to then translate that into

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:52.480
real terms for real people at a jurisdictional,

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:54.340
at a government level,

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.905
um, at a, in a, in a way that can work.

00:33:56.905 --> 00:33:59.830
Um, so that's the story of palm oil.

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:02.065
It is,uh, it is one of

00:34:02.065 --> 00:34:07.030
several examples of how partners are finding a way to work together in

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:10.600
new kinds of collaborations and to continue to work together

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:14.680
as they make progress or face challenges in an evolving partnership,

00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:17.605
um, that is serious about change.

00:34:17.605 --> 00:34:21.385
This is absolutely a team sport.

00:34:21.385 --> 00:34:26.050
Um, and it's a team sport that requires that everyone on the team figure out a way to be

00:34:26.050 --> 00:34:30.920
engaged with each other to figure out a way to forge these kinds of commitments.

00:34:30.920 --> 00:34:33.670
From a business perspective, um,

00:34:33.670 --> 00:34:36.035
there's no better voice on this than Paul Polman who was for

00:34:36.035 --> 00:34:39.035
the last ten years the Chief Executive of Unilever and,

00:34:39.035 --> 00:34:41.135
and really a leader in everything that I've

00:34:41.135 --> 00:34:43.505
talked about from the Consumer Goods Forum Commitment,

00:34:43.505 --> 00:34:45.310
to the Tropical Forests Alliance,

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:48.310
to the development of the jurisdictional approach.

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:51.685
Um, but going back to what Pam said at the beginning,

00:34:51.685 --> 00:34:54.485
what Paul emphasizes is that this is about system change.

00:34:54.485 --> 00:34:57.430
It's about changing the way markets work and

00:34:57.430 --> 00:35:00.565
the responsibilities all of us have as consumers and citizens.

00:35:00.565 --> 00:35:04.810
And in particular, about the role that business has to play in

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:10.220
that larger system to sustain the system that makes their business possible.

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:12.730
Um, and I think there's exciting potential in that.

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:14.480
And I think these are the kinds of efforts

00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:17.680
that will not deliver faster easy solutions but

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:19.570
will be necessary to create

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:23.270
the social capital it takes to succeed in achieving sustainability.

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:27.725
Thank you very much Jim and Pamela for this very informative presentation.

00:35:27.725 --> 00:35:32.120
And now we'll move on to answering your questions.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.600
Okay so the first question we have here is,

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:42.035
what does it take to form a partnership like this and put it in motion?

00:35:42.035 --> 00:35:45.730
Do you want me to start with that? [LAUGHTER].

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:47.410
You're the expert on that. [LAUGHTER].

00:35:47.410 --> 00:35:49.445
Well, I think we both got a lot on that.

00:35:49.445 --> 00:35:51.730
The, the, um, I think it's

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:54.130
important to recognize that the kinds of collaborations we're talking

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:59.545
about are deeply difficult because you're asking people to collaborate,

00:35:59.545 --> 00:36:03.815
not only across organizations with- which is by itself challenging,

00:36:03.815 --> 00:36:05.785
but actually across sectors.

00:36:05.785 --> 00:36:08.500
And when you bring a big NGO, for example,

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:12.025
let's say a big activist NGO together with a big global company,

00:36:12.025 --> 00:36:14.680
you find that you just, you have many,

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:18.030
many differences in basic culture in basic expectations

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:21.525
for how the organization works and the way that you relate to each other and,

00:36:21.525 --> 00:36:23.965
and your expectations for roles in the world.

00:36:23.965 --> 00:36:27.000
So that, so, so making these partnerships work and,

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:28.965
and you would find I'm sure similar things in,

00:36:28.965 --> 00:36:31.589
in building partnerships between competitors,

00:36:31.589 --> 00:36:34.745
right, and which have a different set of, uh, challenges.

00:36:34.745 --> 00:36:36.720
So building these kinds of partnerships, number 1,

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:38.530
takes real commitment on both sides.

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:41.465
This is not a matter of signing an MOU, right?

00:36:41.465 --> 00:36:46.070
This is a matter of real commitment to invest in what it takes to build a partnership.

00:36:46.070 --> 00:36:48.040
And of course, like any relationship,

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:49.430
the heart of that is trust.

00:36:49.430 --> 00:36:50.765
So the heart is, you know,

00:36:50.765 --> 00:36:54.520
do- is making the commitment and making the investment to really

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:58.820
build trust between the two organizations which starts at the top.

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:01.330
I mean, in my experience at WWF, by far,

00:37:01.330 --> 00:37:03.485
the strongest collaborations we had at least at

00:37:03.485 --> 00:37:07.120
a global level were ones where there was a strong engagement,um,

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:11.095
between the CEOs as well as down through the ranks,

00:37:11.095 --> 00:37:15.560
um, so that the signal was clear that the those organizations were serious.

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:18.070
Um, it requires of course then seriousness

00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:20.500
about understanding what the interests really are on

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:23.110
both sides of the conversation and how you can work

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:26.000
together to bring those interests, uh, together.

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.135
So the- there's no magic here, um,

00:37:28.135 --> 00:37:30.730
but it- but it is easily underestimated.

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:34.270
And I think the successes you see in a story like palm oil or

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:37.900
similar stories come out of that kind of commitment from

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:41.380
the top all the way down to really investing in building

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:45.770
understanding and building trust and then being able to make progress.

00:37:45.770 --> 00:37:47.800
Yeah, let me just add to that.

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:52.385
I, I completely agree on the main points that it is trust.

00:37:52.385 --> 00:37:54.770
And, and then the question is how do you build trust?

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:56.195
What does it take to build trust?

00:37:56.195 --> 00:37:58.055
And, and, um, you know,

00:37:58.055 --> 00:38:01.450
how do you- how do we make sure that the partners

00:38:01.450 --> 00:38:04.840
in this are all credible and, and legitimate partners?

00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:09.860
You know, that they're therefore the- at the similar benefits to humankind.

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:15.395
Um, and, um, but I think it is helped by the fact that some of the large,

00:38:15.395 --> 00:38:19.055
uh, non-governmental organizations are really, um,

00:38:19.055 --> 00:38:20.560
leading in a way are,

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:24.710
are working very hard recognizing that it,

00:38:24.710 --> 00:38:28.990
it has to be the- that progress can only be

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:33.575
made if you can develop trust and develop long-term partnerships,

00:38:33.575 --> 00:38:36.605
long-term, um, ah, understanding

00:38:36.605 --> 00:38:40.690
of each other's points of view and, and respect for those.

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:43.465
So along the same lines, um,

00:38:43.465 --> 00:38:45.780
someone asked, regarding the palm oil example,

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:48.070
I was recently at a clean tech conference where they

00:38:48.070 --> 00:38:50.500
mentioned that it is very difficult for governments to actually

00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:52.750
enforce guidelines in umbrella companies

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:55.445
and throughout the supply chain for sustainable palm oil.

00:38:55.445 --> 00:38:59.495
How can one ensure that good intentions are actually followed on and tracked,

00:38:59.495 --> 00:39:03.760
and are there technologies and processes that you have seen to help those?

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:08.980
Yeah. So I would say the- so this is an important point and, and, and, and i- it's,

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:12.055
it's characteristic of a global sector like

00:39:12.055 --> 00:39:14.830
palm oil but also particularly a commodity sector where

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:17.640
the products traded is once it goes into the system that has

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:21.290
historically been, um, ah, invisible.

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:23.755
Um, it's been impossible to know exactly where it came from.

00:39:23.755 --> 00:39:25.555
So I think two things there.

00:39:25.555 --> 00:39:27.085
I mean, one is, um,

00:39:27.085 --> 00:39:29.290
technology is increasingly our friend unless,

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:33.660
and so there's increasing transparency about where deforestation is happening,

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:36.080
and and inc- increasing ability to know,

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:38.110
you know, whose responsibility it is.

00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:40.555
So I think that's a crucial first thing.

00:39:40.555 --> 00:39:44.110
Um, we are increasingly getting the p- power to trace products,

00:39:44.110 --> 00:39:45.755
um, or directly from production,

00:39:45.755 --> 00:39:47.060
all the way through the system.

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:49.865
But then secondly, this is for me a good example of

00:39:49.865 --> 00:39:52.835
the evolution of roles that I was talking about,

00:39:52.835 --> 00:39:54.250
um, a few minutes ago.

00:39:54.250 --> 00:39:56.725
You see that NGOs, who,

00:39:56.725 --> 00:40:00.790
civil society organizations who were in the early days campaigning to, to,

00:40:00.790 --> 00:40:02.450
to get attention to this issue,

00:40:02.450 --> 00:40:05.650
now actually have quite an important role in making sure

00:40:05.650 --> 00:40:09.320
that there's accountability for delivering on the commitments that are made.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:12.820
And when we were- when I was at WWF, for example, many years ago,

00:40:12.820 --> 00:40:16.360
we were periodically issued a scorecard that said here's how

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:21.445
every company is doing in implementing its commitments on, let's say, deforestation.

00:40:21.445 --> 00:40:23.455
And, and a couple of CEO said to me,

00:40:23.455 --> 00:40:25.590
"Could you do- find a way to do that every quarter

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:28.515
because it really keeps people's attention focused on it."

00:40:28.515 --> 00:40:31.930
And,um, I think NGOs have quite an important role in

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:36.010
these phases of a process in being a vehicle for that kind of accountability.

00:40:36.010 --> 00:40:38.020
Can I ask a question on that? [LAUGHTER].

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:38.965
Yeah, sure.

00:40:38.965 --> 00:40:41.500
Just, just I'd love your tho- thoughts on

00:40:41.500 --> 00:40:45.040
blockchain and its role on the accountability on these kinds of things.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:46.295
So I think it's quite impressive there were

00:40:46.295 --> 00:40:49.050
45 minutes into class and we're just now mentioning blockchain.

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:50.405
[LAUGHTER] That's an accomplishment in itself.

00:40:50.405 --> 00:40:51.700
Somebody else probably would have asked. [LAUGHTER].

00:40:51.700 --> 00:40:56.650
Yes, is, um, so I'm not a tech person. Uh, I'm a lawyer.

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:59.920
But one of the things you find is in traceability in discussions is

00:40:59.920 --> 00:41:02.900
that sooner or later everybody mentions blockchain.

00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:04.925
I think the broader concept of

00:41:04.925 --> 00:41:08.945
distributed ledgers clearly has an important role to play, uh,

00:41:08.945 --> 00:41:12.790
in providing a credible and accessible sort of chain of

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:17.240
custody as it were and the ability to track products or whatever.

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:22.060
Um, as [LAUGHTER] Tom Lehrer, the comedian once said,

00:41:22.060 --> 00:41:24.850
"Life is like a sewer and what you'd get out of it depends on what you put

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:27.835
into it," [LAUGHTER] and blockchain is the same way, right?

00:41:27.835 --> 00:41:30.250
Blockchain, it, it can make sure that,

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:33.460
that the train is clear but it can't assure you the quality

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:37.120
of the information input at the beginning or the product input at the beginning.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:40.060
So you still need that complement to what the blockchain can do.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:41.270
Thanks.

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:44.890
Yeah Because I dated myself with that alone.

00:41:44.890 --> 00:41:46.870
[LAUGHTER].

00:41:46.870 --> 00:41:47.950
So next question.

00:41:47.950 --> 00:41:50.320
If given the time and scale of our current challenge,

00:41:50.320 --> 00:41:54.100
we can't afford organizations to take 8-12 years to address issues like this.

00:41:54.100 --> 00:41:57.500
So how can we accelerate this process?

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.440
You want to go first in that?

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:01.390
I'll just say, we have to learn.

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:03.325
Um, you know, I think we,

00:42:03.325 --> 00:42:06.160
and obviously relationships take time,

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:07.990
uh, because they're based on respect,

00:42:07.990 --> 00:42:09.325
they're based on listening, they're,

00:42:09.325 --> 00:42:10.795
they're based on trust, and,

00:42:10.795 --> 00:42:15.835
and so you can't fly in and fly out of a relationship and have trust happen.

00:42:15.835 --> 00:42:17.740
But on the other hand,

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:21.430
I think for anything in the sustainability realm,

00:42:21.430 --> 00:42:24.700
we'd better learn by doing and take home the best,

00:42:24.700 --> 00:42:27.370
ah, lessons and shortcuts,

00:42:27.370 --> 00:42:29.590
and not make the same mistakes twice.

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:31.135
[NOISE] Part of that, part of that,

00:42:31.135 --> 00:42:37.165
uh,14 years was probably having to create some new approaches and,

00:42:37.165 --> 00:42:41.125
and we'll have to continue to do that but we'd better learn. Yeah that.

00:42:41.125 --> 00:42:42.490
No, no I think that's exactly right.

00:42:42.490 --> 00:42:44.770
I mean, somehow, first of all,

00:42:44.770 --> 00:42:46.360
these things are hard and complicated.

00:42:46.360 --> 00:42:47.905
Right? And then I think,

00:42:47.905 --> 00:42:50.705
after this sort of warm glow of commitments,

00:42:50.705 --> 00:42:53.340
we all I think recognize that love.

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:56.280
Actually, translating those into changes is difficult.

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:59.250
And I think we have to make sure that we are from

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:04.850
these first efforts and actually learning how to do it faster and bigger.

00:43:04.850 --> 00:43:07.255
And I think that's a big part of the challenge.

00:43:07.255 --> 00:43:08.530
So in, in interesting way,

00:43:08.530 --> 00:43:12.985
there's a role for the research community in that, you know,

00:43:12.985 --> 00:43:16.600
really paying attention keeping track, analyzing,

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:21.520
evaluating, and sharing what's learned about what's being done.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:24.910
Um, and that, that product of that effort,

00:43:24.910 --> 00:43:28.645
can hopefully be useful to everybody who's engaged in this.

00:43:28.645 --> 00:43:31.180
So, [NOISE] and that not to add on to everything.

00:43:31.180 --> 00:43:34.225
[LAUGHTER] But that's a, that's a rich area for collaboration between

00:43:34.225 --> 00:43:38.950
universities and the civil society sector governments, and especially companies.

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:39.505
And companies.

00:43:39.505 --> 00:43:41.530
Because there's a lot to learn about from

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:43.795
companies who are driving these kinds of changes,

00:43:43.795 --> 00:43:46.330
through their, through their business,

00:43:46.330 --> 00:43:50.425
um, and I think a lot to learn together about how to do that successfully.

00:43:50.425 --> 00:43:53.470
Okay. Thank you. Our next question,

00:43:53.470 --> 00:43:55.720
is what role does social activism play in

00:43:55.720 --> 00:43:58.495
forging these partnerships and kicking off these initiatives.

00:43:58.495 --> 00:44:00.130
For example, would Nestle and Unilever,

00:44:00.130 --> 00:44:04.075
had kept quiet if it weren't for the campaigns mentioned.

00:44:04.075 --> 00:44:09.220
Yes. So, um, I think social activism is, is crucial.

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:12.985
And I think, one of the great strengths of the civil society sector,

00:44:12.985 --> 00:44:16.420
is the diversity of organizations who are able to prove,

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:22.015
to therefore play diverse roles and driving change in a, in a process like this.

00:44:22.015 --> 00:44:23.875
So there's no question that,

00:44:23.875 --> 00:44:26.560
that campaigns were important in,

00:44:26.560 --> 00:44:29.500
in directing attention to this issue and then actually

00:44:29.500 --> 00:44:32.920
galvanizing action by, um, important actors.

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:35.515
Ah, and at the same time, it was crucial,

00:44:35.515 --> 00:44:40.510
that there were big capable conservation organizations in

00:44:40.510 --> 00:44:46.345
the position there help countries and companies translate those commitments into change.

00:44:46.345 --> 00:44:49.000
Um, so that, that ecosystem in itself,

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:51.560
is actually quite an important part of this.

00:44:54.360 --> 00:44:58.090
How can circular economy initiatives contribute to

00:44:58.090 --> 00:45:01.270
the conversation and efforts around sustainability,

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:04.150
beyond its value as a best phrase for marketing and education?

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:12.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:12.820 --> 00:45:15.640
[inaudible] [LAUGHTER] I'm not an expert on the circular economy but I think we can all agree.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:17.035
It's, that's fundamental.

00:45:17.035 --> 00:45:21.490
Right, I mean you, already we are with seven billion people,

00:45:21.490 --> 00:45:24.310
we are straining the capacity of the Earth to support us.

00:45:24.310 --> 00:45:27.745
And of course exceeding that capacity in many places and in many ways.

00:45:27.745 --> 00:45:33.130
Hence, though our numbers and our demand in particular will only grow,

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:37.855
and so clearly we have to have an economy that is less linear.

00:45:37.855 --> 00:45:40.150
Mine it, use it, throw it away,

00:45:40.150 --> 00:45:42.505
um, and, and more circular.

00:45:42.505 --> 00:45:45.775
And that's the key to many of the things we're talking about.

00:45:45.775 --> 00:45:47.485
Yeah. It's interesting when you,

00:45:47.485 --> 00:45:52.135
you look at how human population and consumption has changed.

00:45:52.135 --> 00:45:54.355
Um, we had, ah,

00:45:54.355 --> 00:45:57.670
a billion people on the planet in 1800,

00:45:57.670 --> 00:46:00.190
two billion people in the 1920s,

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:02.680
three billion people in the 1950s.

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:04.720
Today, we have eight plus billion people.

00:46:04.720 --> 00:46:08.800
We're probably going to level off around 10 or 11 billion people.

00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:12.910
We worked like crazy,

00:46:12.910 --> 00:46:17.095
to meet the needs of all those people during that very rapid growth phase,

00:46:17.095 --> 00:46:22.544
and we didn't think about the problems of overusing resources,

00:46:22.544 --> 00:46:24.360
throwing away important products.

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:27.970
We didn't create systems that were circular.

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:29.350
We almost didn't have to,

00:46:29.350 --> 00:46:31.570
and probably we felt like we didn't have time to.

00:46:31.570 --> 00:46:36.340
We were in a mad dash to me the needs are a rapidly growing human population.

00:46:36.340 --> 00:46:39.085
Today, we know what the consequences are,

00:46:39.085 --> 00:46:40.525
today we see the,

00:46:40.525 --> 00:46:42.220
the leveling off of population,

00:46:42.220 --> 00:46:46.510
today we recognize that we can't throw things away anymore,

00:46:46.510 --> 00:46:48.325
that we have to use our resources,

00:46:48.325 --> 00:46:50.770
um, much more intelligently.

00:46:50.770 --> 00:46:52.495
And, ah, so the circle,

00:46:52.495 --> 00:46:57.505
a circular economy initiatives are a huge prospective of a,

00:46:57.505 --> 00:47:00.910
a huge factor in doing that.

00:47:00.910 --> 00:47:05.260
It's not just using things more efficiently, reducing use but,

00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:08.530
but and changing the kinds of materials we use, but,

00:47:08.530 --> 00:47:13.195
but making sure that they are, um, never wasted.

00:47:13.195 --> 00:47:17.110
So it's a, it's an exciting time. I think for that.

00:47:17.110 --> 00:47:20.590
Right. Okay. The next question is,

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:24.160
how can we engage corporations and form these partnerships in

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:28.300
regions where civil activism and education around sustainability is lacking?

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:32.140
[NOISE].

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:35.110
Well, it's funny [LAUGHTER] partly, if partly,

00:47:35.110 --> 00:47:38.710
um, anything that saves money, ah,

00:47:38.710 --> 00:47:40.150
and that actually is,

00:47:40.150 --> 00:47:42.310
is addressing the needs of people,

00:47:42.310 --> 00:47:47.035
um, in areas where there isn't any activism are still gonna be drivers for this.

00:47:47.035 --> 00:47:48.550
But the interesting thing about,

00:47:48.550 --> 00:47:50.515
about Jim's case study is that,

00:47:50.515 --> 00:47:53.830
you see a, a top-down, ah, force,

00:47:53.830 --> 00:47:57.970
that is affecting what's happening in places where there isn't

00:47:57.970 --> 00:48:02.950
activism or where there isn't a community based interests and sustainability.

00:48:02.950 --> 00:48:06.790
Um, it's a global world and it's a global problem.

00:48:06.790 --> 00:48:10.360
Um, but I also think that the recognition,

00:48:10.360 --> 00:48:14.830
that there are real benefits to doing things differently, [LAUGHTER] that there are,

00:48:14.830 --> 00:48:17.500
there are benefits in terms of meeting the needs of people,

00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:20.290
saving money, um, and, ah,

00:48:20.290 --> 00:48:24.280
and in many other areas make it, um,

00:48:24.280 --> 00:48:26.005
that alone a driver,

00:48:26.005 --> 00:48:29.830
even if people don't put the sustainability term on it.

00:48:29.830 --> 00:48:32.200
Yeah. I think the only thing I'd add to it is, I,

00:48:32.200 --> 00:48:35.020
I think a fundamental principle to any offer like this,

00:48:35.020 --> 00:48:38.410
is that you, you have to be able to frame issues in

00:48:38.410 --> 00:48:41.830
terms of resonate with the audience and in the place that you're talking about.

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:43.255
Hence, and that means,

00:48:43.255 --> 00:48:45.940
you know, so for example, in,

00:48:45.940 --> 00:48:51.250
hu- human health has been a much better entry point for many of these issues,

00:48:51.250 --> 00:48:55.300
then force protection or biodiversity conservation lies in many markets.

00:48:55.300 --> 00:48:58.000
And, and you need to be able to talk and human health terms or what is,

00:48:58.000 --> 00:48:59.500
why is this important for human health?

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:01.810
And you need to be able to reach people on those terms.

00:49:01.810 --> 00:49:04.240
So to take it for China for example,

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:07.330
China is the largest buyer of many commodities around the world,

00:49:07.330 --> 00:49:09.910
but Chinese consumers are increasingly

00:49:09.910 --> 00:49:12.910
concerned and particularly with food, about food safety.

00:49:12.910 --> 00:49:14.905
And that takes them traceability.

00:49:14.905 --> 00:49:19.165
That takes them to wanting to know more about how the things they buy were produced.

00:49:19.165 --> 00:49:21.280
And so the entry point in China on these issues

00:49:21.280 --> 00:49:23.635
may well be through traceability in food safety as,

00:49:23.635 --> 00:49:26.305
as opposed to initially through force protection.

00:49:26.305 --> 00:49:28.360
But in each case, it's a matter of what,

00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:31.060
what are people worried about and how are the,

00:49:31.060 --> 00:49:33.670
how do you frame this issue in a way that will resonate.

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:35.185
Like wise, you know,

00:49:35.185 --> 00:49:37.420
many farmers around the world who would like to

00:49:37.420 --> 00:49:40.495
save money on the inputs they use in agriculture,

00:49:40.495 --> 00:49:42.490
it costs a lot to fertilize.

00:49:42.490 --> 00:49:44.125
Ah, but if, ah,

00:49:44.125 --> 00:49:45.970
at the same time by doing so,

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:49.945
by reducing how much fertilizer you have to use, there're tremendous,

00:49:49.945 --> 00:49:52.165
ah, benefits for the environment,

00:49:52.165 --> 00:49:53.710
and so it's a win-win,

00:49:53.710 --> 00:49:56.030
and there are lots of win-wins.

00:49:57.690 --> 00:50:00.730
Our next question is, how can you contribute to

00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:05.240
such initiatives and create social capital at the individual level?

00:50:05.460 --> 00:50:08.125
Yeah. This is it.

00:50:08.125 --> 00:50:12.100
It in basically every part of one's life, you can contribute.

00:50:12.100 --> 00:50:17.815
I think, and we have this conversation a lot in an educational institution like Stanford.

00:50:17.815 --> 00:50:20.710
How can we as individuals, how can we as a,

00:50:20.710 --> 00:50:22.720
as a, as a community,

00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:24.295
um, make a big difference?

00:50:24.295 --> 00:50:27.550
And, uh, simply by the choices of what we eat,

00:50:27.550 --> 00:50:28.930
and what we drive,

00:50:28.930 --> 00:50:30.670
and, and how we, um,

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:34.284
how we think about our resource use, etc,

00:50:34.284 --> 00:50:36.955
we contribute individually and,

00:50:36.955 --> 00:50:39.370
um, we by doing so,

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:43.210
can build a social movement among our communities,

00:50:43.210 --> 00:50:45.220
among our friends, among our families.

00:50:45.220 --> 00:50:47.530
That is a very much a bottom-up.

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:51.565
I think there is another question about how we as individuals can, can,

00:50:51.565 --> 00:50:53.755
can create change at a,

00:50:53.755 --> 00:50:56.875
at a scale that affects more communities.

00:50:56.875 --> 00:50:59.485
And that might be through your company,

00:50:59.485 --> 00:51:03.100
driving, driving change in a company or in an,

00:51:03.100 --> 00:51:05.665
in an organization, encouraging, um,

00:51:05.665 --> 00:51:09.880
people to think more systematically about what's needed.

00:51:09.880 --> 00:51:13.780
Uh, becoming systems thinkers I think is a key to that.

00:51:13.780 --> 00:51:15.940
Yeah, I, I, I agree with that.

00:51:15.940 --> 00:51:18.100
Interest to translate that into the palm oil case.

00:51:18.100 --> 00:51:20.095
I mean, the, the first of all,

00:51:20.095 --> 00:51:21.520
when we talk about what can people do,

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:23.350
people usually think first about voting and

00:51:23.350 --> 00:51:25.720
voting is crucial right in any part of the world.

00:51:25.720 --> 00:51:28.030
Right, voting really matters.

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:31.240
Because in the end, governments are and have to be at the center of this.

00:51:31.240 --> 00:51:34.810
But one of the things you see in a case like palm oil is that consumers,

00:51:34.810 --> 00:51:37.495
the decisions you make everyday in the grocery store,

00:51:37.495 --> 00:51:39.490
the vote you cast and the way you,

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:41.620
in the products you choose really matters.

00:51:41.620 --> 00:51:43.030
And, you know, when you,

00:51:43.030 --> 00:51:46.240
you look at the IRA glass that I showed and those 500 companies,

00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:50.395
it's easy to feel maybe disempowered while it's superimposed companies that matter.

00:51:50.395 --> 00:51:52.840
But those companies are listening to you.

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:57.160
Right, I mean, those companies are very attuned to what their consumers,

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:58.690
their customers are thinking.

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:00.235
And so how you,

00:52:00.235 --> 00:52:01.495
what you do in the marketplace,

00:52:01.495 --> 00:52:03.790
even if you do it quietly, really matters.

00:52:03.790 --> 00:52:05.410
And if beyond that,

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:07.150
you ask your retailer, so, you know,

00:52:07.150 --> 00:52:12.580
what are you doing about palm oil or about seafood or about those messages get back?

00:52:12.580 --> 00:52:14.280
So I think then you can actually do

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:16.425
all the thing being talking about the choices you make,

00:52:16.425 --> 00:52:20.745
really do a ripple through the system to these large levers.

00:52:20.745 --> 00:52:25.350
Great, thank you. Unfortunately, we're getting close to the hour.

00:52:25.350 --> 00:52:27.960
So we'll have to wrap up the Q&amp;A session.

00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:30.150
Thank you very much Jim and Pamela for

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:32.370
sharing your knowledge with our participants today.

